course: dictionary
lesson: 11263
title: A fish that cleans other fish
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: You may not know the word wrasse, but the clues in the sentences help. «Cleaner wrasse pick parasites and dead skin off big fish, even inside their mouths.» Pick off and dead skin tell you the wrasse is removing things from the big fish, not eating it.
explain: Read on for more clues. «A cleaner wrasse swimming into a big fish's mouth and coming out safe» shows the wrasse goes in and comes out unharmed, so it must be small and careful. Swap in your meaning: "a small fish that cleans bigger fish" still makes sense in every sentence.
explain: Clues can also show where a word lives. «Reefs are the busiest neighbourhoods in the sea» tells you reefs are in the sea, and the wrasse lives there too. When the clues are not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: A wrasse is a brightly coloured sea fish with thick lips.
fact: Wrasse live in warm seas, around rocky coasts and coral reefs.
fact: A «cleaner wrasse» is a small wrasse that eats tiny pests off bigger fish.
fact: «Ballan wrasse» live around British coasts and rock pools.
fact: Many wrasse can change from female to male.
exercise: What is a wrasse? | A plant that grows on rocks | A type of coral | *A brightly coloured sea fish with thick lips | A bird that sings over the reef
exercise: «A cleaner wrasse runs a cleaning station for big fish.» What does a cleaner wrasse eat? | Coral, which it crunches into sand | Whole big fish | Seaweed from the rocks | *Tiny pests, such as parasites, off bigger fish
exercise: Where would you find a ballan wrasse? | *Around British coasts and in rock pools | Inside a desert sand dune | High up on a mountain path | In a city park pond
test: «The big fish does not eat its cleaner, so both win.» What does both win mean? | Both fish lose | Only the big fish wins | *Both fish get something good | Only the wrasse wins
test: «Reefs are the busiest neighbourhoods in the sea.» What does busiest suggest? | Empty and silent | *Full of activity | Cold and dark | Very deep
exam: Which fish is a cleaner wrasse? | *A small wrasse that eats tiny pests off bigger fish | A large shark that hunts seals | A clownfish that lives in an anemone | A parrotfish that crunches coral
